// issue #2558: `+` over two GENERIC calls inside a provide method — the bug's
// own spelling, `id("hi ") + id(who)`. The component dialect spells a call to a
// module `fn` as a `{"kind": "fn", "name", "args"}` node, not the `call` node
// the module-fn spine writes, so before this issue neither `_v3_is_str` nor
// `_v3_infer_type` recognised it: each operand read as the callee's DECLARED
// return `T` with nothing bound to it, the `+` was rendered as the arithmetic
// `(id(..) + id(..))`, and cargo answered E0308 because `String + String` does
// not exist. Bound from the arguments, each side is a `Str`, so the `+` is a
// concatenation and renders `format!("{}{}", .., ..)`.
//
// Every method below reaches the seam only through the `fn` node, so none of
// them built before the fix:
//   `hello`   — two generic calls added to each other, the reported spelling;
//   `nearest` — a generic call added to a `Str` PARAMETER, then to a provide-body
//               `let` that is itself such a sum. The parameter is typed by
//               `var_types`, not by either arm, so this one is the control: it
//               worked before #2558 and must keep working after it;
//   `shout`   — a NON-generic `Str`-returning call (`upper`) added to a generic
//               one. `_v3_is_str`'s new `fn` arm answers this from the declared
//               return alone, with no binding, which is the branch that would
//               silently regress if the binding ever became mandatory.
fn id[T](x: T) -> T { return x }

fn upper(s: Str) -> Str { return s }

service Greeter {
  fn hello(who: Str) -> Str
  fn nearest(with_str: Str) -> Str
  fn shout(who: Str) -> Str
}

component Hello provides greeting: Greeter {
  provide greeting {
    fn hello(who) {
      return id("hi ") + id(who)
    }
    fn nearest(with_str) {
      let both = id(with_str) + id("there")
      return id("dear ") + both
    }
    fn shout(who) {
      return upper("hi ") + id(who)
    }
  }
}
